# AUTHOR LINE GROUPING — the NESTED cell: a MULTI-LINE element on a row.
#
# MUST EXCLUDE from any bulk `gg fmt` sweep: this fixture is a deliberately
# UNFORMATTED INPUT — the assertion is on what `gg fmt` turns it INTO
# (`fmt_author_rows_multiline_elem_shares_row`, exact text). A sweep would
# rewrite it into its own expected output and the cell would go green-vacuous.
#
# AXIS = grouping shape, NESTED cell. The outer list is exploded by its own
# magic comma and hand-grouped into two rows. Its FIRST element is an inner list
# that is itself exploded by its own magic comma, and the author put `[3, 4]` on
# the same line as the inner list's closing bracket.
#
# ⚠ WHAT THIS PINS. The row decision is made from the AUTHOR's source spans, and
# it is made BEFORE any element is rendered — so an element whose own render
# spans several output lines does not disturb the assignment of its NEIGHBOURS.
# The inner list keeps its own rows for its own reason (the propagation is
# per-container, exactly as it is for the magic comma), and the outer row that
# STARTS with it still carries `[3, 4]`.
#
# The result is a row whose first element is many lines tall. That reads oddly
# and is nonetheless right: the author asked for it, and the alternative — a
# rule that silently re-flows a row containing a multi-line element — is a
# second layout dimension with no ratification behind it.
#
# RED against the pre-change binary, measured: not a fixed point — the outer
# list comes back with each of its three elements on its own line.
from std.collections import Vector

void main():
    int y = 2
    Vector[Vector[int]] xs = [
        [1,
         y,], [3, 4],
        [5, 6],
    ]
    print(xs.len())
